A set of logical connectives is functionally complete if every possible truth function can be expressed using only those connectives. Since $\{
eg, \land, \lor\}$ is known to be complete, we only need to show that $\land$ can be expressed using $\{
eg, \lor\}$. Using De Morgan's Law: $p \land q \equiv 
eg(
eg p \lor 
eg q)$. Since any expression can be reduced to one using $
eg$ and $\land$, and we can replace $\land$ with $
eg$ and $\lor$, the set $\{
eg, \lor\}$ is sufficient to express any formula.
